# macOS Glass Design

AirScript should feel like a native macOS app that uses real system glass — not a web-style frosted overlay.

## Philosophy

- **Material over paint.** Backgrounds are `Material` (and `NSVisualEffectView` only if SwiftUI materials are insufficient). Do not fake blur with opacity stacks or bitmap frosts.
- **Content on glass, not glass on noise.** Keep surfaces quiet. Hierarchy comes from material thickness, spacing, and typography — not heavy shadows or neon borders.
- **System first.** Semantic colors (`Color.primary`, `.secondary`, `.tertiary`), SF Symbols, San Francisco via SwiftUI text styles. No custom window traffic lights.
- **Vibrancy.** Foreground must remain legible as the desktop shows through. Prefer `.foregroundStyle(.primary/.secondary)` over hardcoded hex.
- **Light and dark.** Tokens are semantic so both appearances work. Never assume a dark-only glass look.
- **Respect Reduce Transparency.** When `accessibilityReduceTransparency` is on, use opaque `Color(nsColor: .windowBackgroundColor)` instead of materials.
- **Native chrome.** `NavigationSplitView` sidebars, inspectors, toolbars, and continuous corners. Do not reinvent panels that AppKit already provides.

## Tokens

Define these once in `DesignSystem/Tokens.swift` and reference them — never scatter magic numbers.

### Spacing (4 pt grid)

`xxs 4 · xs 8 · sm 12 · md 16 · lg 24 · xl 32 · xxl 48`

### Radius

`control 6 · row 8 · card 12 · panel 16` — always `RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius:style: .continuous)`

### Material

| Role | Token |
| --- | --- |
| Overlay / popover | `.ultraThinMaterial` |
| Card / editor canvas | `.thinMaterial` |
| Sidebar / inspector | `.regularMaterial` |
| Dense chrome | `.thickMaterial` |

### Stroke & shadow

- Hairline: `Color.white.opacity(0.18)` (dark) / `Color.black.opacity(0.08)` (light) — use `Color.primary.opacity(0.08)` if you need one semantic stroke.
- Shadow: `color: .black.opacity(0.12), radius: 12, y: 4` — one soft lift, never stacked drop shadows.
- Glass fill: material only. Optional inner highlight: 1 pt stroke, not a second fill.

### Type

Use text styles, then weight: `.largeTitle` app hero, `.title2` window/section, `.headline` row title, `.body` content, `.callout` / `.caption` meta. Line length stays readable; do not center large paragraphs.

### Control size

Toolbar and list: `.controlSize(.regular)`. Dense inspectors may use `.small`. Touch-style oversized buttons are not macOS.

## Implementation pattern

```swift
struct GlassPanel<Content: View>: View {
    @Environment(\.accessibilityReduceTransparency) private var reduceTransparency
    var content: () -> Content

    var body: some View {
        content()
            .padding(DS.Spacing.md)
            .background {
                if reduceTransparency {
                    RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: DS.Radius.panel, style: .continuous)
                        .fill(Color(nsColor: .windowBackgroundColor))
                } else {
                    RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: DS.Radius.panel, style: .continuous)
                        .fill(.thinMaterial)
                }
            }
            .overlay {
                RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: DS.Radius.panel, style: .continuous)
                    .strokeBorder(.primary.opacity(0.08), lineWidth: 1)
            }
    }
}
```

## Do / don't

- Do: translucent sidebar, inset grouped lists, vibrancy-aware labels, SF Symbol + title toolbar items.
- Don't: CSS-like `blur + white 0.2 fill` everywhere, rainbow gradients, 24 pt shadows, iOS-only tab bars as the primary desktop nav, opaque cards that kill the glass.
